sv.c, sv.h, sv_inline.h, gv.h, pp.c, pp_ctl.c: every SV head a Sublet child
lifetime of its arena, under PERL_CAPSTONE_SUBLET (5.36.3). Without the define
the preprocessed source is unchanged.

Upstream carves SV heads from 4080-byte arenas and keeps free ones on
PL_sv_root, a list threaded through the freed heads. A stale SV* therefore
reaches whatever value the head is handed out to next, and the memory never
returns to malloc, so neither the system allocator nor ASan sees the free.

Two instructions do the work. CDERIVE makes a child capability of a
non-linear parent, bounded to a sub-range; CREVOKE(parent, child) ends a
direct child and everything below it.

- Perl_more_sv allocates the arena with a trailer: the child each head was
  handed out as. The arena's first slot, which is not an SV, keeps the arena's
  lifetime (a CDERIVE of the arena) in its sv_u.
- uproot_SV, the one path to a new head (new_SV and Perl_more_sv), hands the
  head out as a CDERIVE child of the arena's lifetime bounded to the head, and
  records it in the trailer.
- plant_SV finds the head's arena by address, revokes the recorded child
  (CREVOKE) and works on the head through the arena's own pointer, which is
  what the free list holds. An SVf_BREAK head is not revoked: upstream keeps
  it off the free list because pointers to it are expected to outlive it.
- The core reads freed heads by design (sv.h: "there are some parts of the
  core that have pointers to already-freed SV heads"). SvIS_FREED, GvIO's type
  test and the stash probes in pp_caller, the eval compiler and pp_anonconst
  read the head through the arena's own pointer at the same address, never
  through the possibly stale one. That reads what upstream reads there: the
  freed flags, or the head's next value.

Upstream's policy is unchanged: the same LIFO list, the same arena geometry
and growth, so the same heads are handed out in the same order. One
interpreter only (no MULTIPLICITY). Needs capstone/runtime/include on the
include path (capstone/capability.h) and the virtual profile.

